// src/user/aml/aml_region.pdx — R30.M3-002 (#1062)
//
// The SystemMemory address-space handler, and the point at which this
// subsystem stops being pure computation over a byte buffer.
//
// =====================================================================
// THE MAPPING / ACCESS SPLIT — and why the corpus can be honest
// =====================================================================
// Every earlier module in src/user/aml/ could be tested exhaustively
// because it touched nothing but its own arenas. A region handler
// cannot claim that: its whole purpose is to read and write addresses
// that live outside the process.
//
// So the module is cut in two, along the line the security argument
// already runs along:
//
//   THE MAPPING STEP — `aml_region_bind`. Takes a capability window
//   {win_base, win_len} and the host address that window is mapped at,
//   checks the firmware-declared region against it, and records a
//   binding. This step decides WHETHER an address may be touched and
//   WHERE it lives. It performs no access.
//
//   THE ACCESS STEP — `aml_region_read_unit` / `aml_region_write_unit`
//   and the field machinery above them. Takes a BINDING INDEX and a
//   byte offset. It has no way to name an address: the base it uses is
//   the one the mapping step recorded, and the length it clips to is the
//   one the mapping step validated.
//
// The corpus therefore exercises the REAL access step — the real bounds
// arithmetic, the real access-width selection, the real read-modify-
// write — against a synthetic backing buffer, by handing the mapping
// step a `host_va` that points at that buffer instead of at mapped
// device memory. Not one line of the code under test differs between
// the two cases. No fixture in this repository ever names a real
// physical address, and none needs to: the part that would differ is the
// part that does no arithmetic.
//
// HOW THE SPLIT FALLS FOR EACH SPACE (#1063 / #1064 / #1065):
//
//   SystemMemory — transaction: a sized load/store at host_base + off.
//   Logic: everything else. The corpus supplies host_va.
//
//   PCI_Config — transaction: THE SAME sized load/store, because an ECAM
//   access IS a memory access. This is why the space needs no new
//   transaction primitive at all and why opregion_space_base_kind maps
//   it to KIND_MEMORY. What is NEW here is pure logic and therefore
//   fully testable: resolving {seg, bus, dev, func} from the namespace,
//   bounding the offset by config-space size, and resolving AnyAcc to
//   dword. The corpus points host_va at a 4096-byte synthetic function
//   and exercises all of it.
//
//   SystemIO — transaction: `in`/`out`, confined to aml_region_port_in
//   and aml_region_port_out, the only two functions in this repository
//   containing those instructions. Logic: identical to the memory path.
//   The corpus runs the logic against a synthetic backing AND separately
//   proves, by the #GP it takes in ring 3, that the real-sentinel path
//   reaches a genuine port instruction. See those functions.
//
//   EmbeddedControl — transaction: NONE, gated on R31. Logic: bind-time
//   validation, byte-only width resolution, and the refusal itself.
//   The corpus exercises the logic and pins the transaction at zero.
//
// =====================================================================
// WHY A MISSING CAPABILITY CANNOT BE BYPASSED
// =====================================================================
// The threat is concrete. A DSDT is vendor-supplied, unsigned, and can
// declare `OperationRegion(FOO, SystemMemory, <any 64-bit address>,
// <any length>)`. Nothing in the AML language stops that address from
// naming kernel text, the page tables, another driver's DMA buffer, or
// this process's own object arena.
//
// Four properties, together, make "forgot to check the capability" not
// expressible here:
//
//   1. THE ACCESS STEP TAKES NO ADDRESS. `aml_region_read_unit(b, off,
//      n)` computes `host_base(b) + off`. There is no entry point in
//      this module that accepts a raw pointer to read or write. A caller
//      that has not been through `aml_region_bind` has nothing to pass.
//
//   2. `host_base` HAS EXACTLY ONE WRITER. Only `aml_region_bind` ever
//      stores to a row, and it refuses before storing if the capability
//      handle is zero, if the window length is zero, if the host address
//      is zero, or if the declared range is not contained in the window.
//      There is no "bind unchecked" variant, because the checked one is
//      the only one.
//
//   3. THE STORAGE IS CONFINED. `aml_region_tab` and
//      `aml_region_state` are asserted by tools/verify-aml-parser.sh
//      (via `objdump -r`) to be relocated against from aml_region.o and
//      no other object. A module that reached the table directly could
//      fabricate a row; none can.
//
//   4. THE LIVENESS TEST IS THE LOOKUP. Every access path begins with
//      `aml_region_row_live`, which requires a non-zero node AND a
//      non-zero capability handle AND a non-zero host base. A row that
//      failed any gate in `aml_region_bind` was never written, so it
//      fails the liveness test as a free row does, and the access is
//      refused with AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND rather than proceeding
//      against zeroes.
//
// The kernel half of the argument is in src/kernel/core/cap/
// kind_op_region.pdx: there is no kernel path that yields a
// KIND_OP_REGION covering a range the caller did not already hold, and
// the window this module is handed comes out of that capability's own
// row — never out of the firmware table.
//
// REFUSAL, NEVER TRUNCATION. A declared region that exceeds its backing
// capability is refused at BIND time with AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED.
// It is not clipped to the covered part. Clipping would let a table
// declare a huge region, observe which offsets succeed, and thereby map
// out exactly what the supervisor holds — an address oracle that never
// has to be told "no". Refusal leaks one bit; truncation leaks the
// boundary.
//
// A region declared with no covering capability at all — bind called
// with a zero handle or a zero-length window — is AML_ERR_REGION_NO_CAP.
// That is the headline refusal of this milestone and it happens before
// any address is formed.
//
// =====================================================================
// ACCESS WIDTH IS DECLARED, NOT CHOSEN
// =====================================================================
// An AML `Field` declaration carries an AccessType — ByteAcc, WordAcc,
// DWordAcc, QWordAcc, AnyAcc — and it is not a hint. Real hardware
// registers latch on the transaction width: servicing a DWordAcc field
// with four byte reads can return four snapshots of a register that
// changed between them, and servicing a ByteAcc field with a dword write
// can touch three neighbouring registers with side effects. The
// declaration exists because the table author knows something the
// interpreter does not.
//
// So `aml_region_acc_bits` resolves the declared type to the exact
// transaction width used for EVERY unit access of that field:
//
//   ByteAcc  -> 8      WordAcc  -> 16
//   DWordAcc -> 32     QWordAcc -> 64
//   AnyAcc   -> 8      BufferAcc -> refused
//
// AnyAcc resolves to 8 for SystemMemory. AnyAcc means "the interpreter
// may choose", and choosing the NARROWEST is always inside the
// permission granted — a byte access to system memory is always legal,
// where a dword access to a field near the end of a region may not fit.
// The resolver is a table rather than a branch precisely so that
// R30.M3-004's PCI_Config handler, where dword is the natural unit and
// AnyAcc must resolve to 32, becomes a row here instead of a special
// case somewhere else.
//
// BufferAcc is refused with AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH: it is the
// SMBus / GenericSerialBus / IPMI protocol form, where the "access" is a
// bus transaction carrying a buffer, and it has no meaning over a memory
// window. Silently treating it as ByteAcc would run an SMBus protocol
// field as raw memory.
//
// =====================================================================
// BIT-GRANULAR FIELDS AND READ-MODIFY-WRITE
// =====================================================================
// Fields are bit-granular and need not be aligned to anything. A
// DWordAcc field at bit offset 3 with width 40 spans two dwords, and
// both halves must be assembled from ALIGNED dword transactions at the
// declared width — never from a narrower access that happens to land
// where the bits are.
//
// The read loop walks the field's bit extent one access unit at a time:
// for each unit it computes the intersection of the field with that
// unit, reads the WHOLE unit at the declared width, extracts the
// intersecting bits, and appends them to the result at the running
// output bit position.
//
// The write loop is the same walk with the UpdateRule (§19.5.5.2)
// deciding what happens to the bits of a touched unit the field does not
// cover:
//
//   Preserve (0)      read the unit, replace only the field's bits,
//                     write it back. This is the read-modify-write, and
//                     it is the default.
//   WriteAsOnes (1)   uncovered bits become 1; NO read is performed.
//   WriteAsZeros (2)  uncovered bits become 0; NO read is performed.
//
// The suppressed read under rules 1 and 2 is deliberate and is the
// reason those rules exist: they name registers where reading has side
// effects (clear-on-read status bits, FIFO pops). An implementation that
// read anyway "to be safe" would corrupt exactly the hardware the rule
// was written to protect.
//
// =====================================================================
// STRADDLING IS AN ERROR, NOT A NARROWER ACCESS
// =====================================================================
// If an access unit at the declared width would extend past the end of
// the region, the access is refused with AML_ERR_REGION_OOB. It is not
// wrapped, not partially completed, and not silently narrowed to the
// bytes that fit.
//
// Narrowing would be the tempting repair — the bits the field wants are
// inside the region, after all. It is wrong twice: it changes the
// transaction width the table declared (see above), and it means the
// region's length no longer bounds what a table can reach with a
// well-chosen field offset. A field that straddles the end of its region
// is a table bug, and reporting it at the access is the only place with
// enough context to say which field.
//
// =====================================================================
// THE FIELDUNIT BOUNDARY MOVES HERE
// =====================================================================
// R30.M2-004 (#1057) left every FieldUnit load and store refused with
// AML_ERR_BAD_TARGET (41), with the note that "writing one is a bus
// transaction and belongs to R30.M3's region handlers". This issue is
// that milestone, and the boundary moves.
//
// NOW REAL: a load or store of a FieldUnit whose enclosing `Field`
// names a SystemMemory OperationRegion that has been BOUND to a
// capability window. Reads assemble from aligned units at the declared
// width; writes read-modify-write under the UpdateRule.
//
// THE BOUNDARY AFTER #1063 / #1064 / #1065, stated so the next issue
// inherits a line and not a guess:
//
//   * SystemMemory, SystemIO, PCI_Config and EmbeddedControl are
//     SERVICED. Each is a row in `aml_region_space_supported` and a row
//     in `aml_region_acc_bits`, not a branch somewhere else.
//   * AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE (62) is now narrow enough to ENUMERATE. It
//     means exactly: SMBus (4), SystemCMOS (5), PciBarTarget (6),
//     IPMI (7), GeneralPurposeIO (8), GenericSerialBus (9), PCC (10),
//     and every vendor-defined space at 0x80 and above. All seven are
//     bus protocols whose "access" is a transaction with a command and a
//     reply, not a load from an offset. SystemCMOS is the one that looks
//     serviceable — it is reached through ports 0x70/0x71 — but it is an
//     INDEX/DATA pair, so offset N is not port 0x70+N, and running it on
//     the SystemIO path would write the index register with data.
//   * EmbeddedControl BINDS but does not TRANSACT: every EC access is
//     AML_ERR_REGION_EC_GATED (67) until R31's EC driver registers a
//     backing. Nothing is simulated. See the EC section below.
//
// THE THREE SPACES, AND THE ONE HAZARD EACH:
//
//   * SystemIO — the RMW hazard is sharper here than anywhere else. A
//     port read can clear a status bit, pop a FIFO or advance a latch, so
//     `Preserve` on a partial field write does real damage that a memory
//     region would not suffer. The UpdateRule machinery #1062 built is
//     honoured unchanged on the port path, and the corpus asserts the
//     ACCESS COUNT, not just the resulting bytes, because "did it read?"
//     is the entire question. What a `Preserve` partial write to a port
//     does is: exactly one read of the unit, then exactly one write —
//     no more, and never zero. That is what the table asked for, and
//     when it is the wrong thing for a port, the table's own remedy is
//     to declare WriteAsOnes or WriteAsZeros, which suppress the read.
//     The interpreter does not second-guess that choice; it obeys it
//     exactly and makes the obedience observable.
//
//   * PCI_Config — the hazard is not a fault but SILENT CORRUPTION. The
//     region carries no bus/device/function; it inherits them from the
//     enclosing Device's _ADR and the enclosing host bridge's _BBN/_SEG.
//     Resolve it wrong and every bounds check still passes while another
//     device's BARs are rewritten. So: refuse, never default. See
//     aml_region_pci_context.
//
//   * EmbeddedControl — the hazard is FABRICATION. A handler that
//     returned plausible bytes would make thermal and battery code look
//     healthy while reading fiction.
//   * A FieldUnit under an IndexField or a BankField is
//     AML_ERR_REGION_INDIRECT_FIELD (64). Those are two-step protocols
//     — write an index register, then access a data register — and the
//     step is a real access with its own ordering requirements. Faking
//     it as a direct offset would silently read the wrong register.
//   * A field wider than 64 bits is AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH (61).
//     ACPI says such a field reads as a Buffer, not an Integer; the
//     Buffer-valued form needs the object model's heap and is R30.M4.
//     Truncating to 64 bits would return a plausible wrong answer.
//   * A FieldUnit whose region was never bound is
//     AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND (58) — the refusal that catches "the
//     supervisor never obtained a capability for this region".
//   * AML_ERR_BAD_TARGET (41) keeps its remaining meaning: a store
//     destination that names no cell at all.
//
// =====================================================================
// FUEL
// =====================================================================
// Every unit access spends one unit of evaluator fuel, via the same
// `aml_eval_spend` the interpreter's termination guarantee rests on. A
// field spanning K access units costs K, not 1: the cost model has to
// track the work, or a `While` loop over a wide unaligned field becomes
// a way to buy unbounded hardware transactions with a bounded fuel
// budget. `aml_region_accesses` counts the same events so the corpus can
// assert the two agree.
//
// =====================================================================
// STORAGE
// =====================================================================
// 16 rows of 8 u64s. Row 0 is the null sentinel — a binding index of 0
// means "no binding", the same convention the arenas use, which is what
// lets `aml_region_find` return a bare index with no separate presence
// flag. 15 usable bindings; a real DSDT declares on the order of a dozen
// OperationRegions. Exhaustion is a clean latched
// AML_ERR_REGION_TABLE_FULL (63), never an overwrite.

module AmlRegion = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // Error codes — continuing the shared code space (#1054 §E). A code
  // still identifies its origin unambiguously.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_NO_CAP         : u64 = 56
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED    : u64 = 57
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND        : u64 = 58
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_OOB            : u64 = 59
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH   : u64 = 60
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH    : u64 = 61
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE          : u64 = 62
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_TABLE_FULL     : u64 = 63
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_INDIRECT_FIELD : u64 = 64
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_PORT_RANGE     : u64 = 65
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_PCI_CONTEXT    : u64 = 66
  pub let AML_ERR_REGION_EC_GATED       : u64 = 67

  // ==========================================================================
  // Address spaces this handler services. #1062 opened with one row;
  // #1063, #1064 and #1065 add the other three.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_MEMORY : u64 = 0x00
  pub let AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_IO     : u64 = 0x01
  pub let AML_SPACE_PCI_CONFIG    : u64 = 0x02
  pub let AML_SPACE_EC            : u64 = 0x03

  pub let AML_REGION_MAX  : u64 = 16
  pub let AML_REGION_WORDS: u64 = 8

  // ==========================================================================
  // Space-specific bounds and sentinels — #1063 / #1064 / #1065.
  // ==========================================================================

  // The x86 I/O port space is 16 bits wide. This is not a policy limit
  // that a later revision might raise: `in`/`out` take the port in DX and
  // there is no wider form of the instruction, so a port above 0xFFFF is
  // not merely unauthorised, it is unaddressable.
  pub let AML_PORT_MAX : u64 = 0xFFFF

  // host_va sentinel meaning "this SystemIO binding issues REAL port
  // transactions". Any other non-zero host_va is a mapped byte buffer
  // that stands in for the port range, which is how the corpus exercises
  // every line of the port path except the one instruction that differs.
  //
  // 1 is chosen because it is not a valid mapping: the zero page is never
  // mapped in this system, so no synthetic backing can ever collide with
  // the sentinel, and a caller that means "real ports" cannot accidentally
  // be handed a buffer that reads as the sentinel.
  pub let AML_REGION_IO_REAL : u64 = 1

  // PCI configuration space per function: 256 bytes on the legacy
  // mechanism, 4096 with ECAM. A PCI_Config OperationRegion's offset is
  // an offset INTO config space, never an address.
  pub let AML_PCI_CFG_LEGACY : u64 = 256
  pub let AML_PCI_CFG_EXT    : u64 = 4096

  // The EC address space is 256 bytes of EC RAM, addressed one byte at a
  // time by the EC protocol (ACPI 6.5 §12.3). There is no wider EC
  // transaction, which is why ByteAcc is the only access type admitted.
  pub let AML_EC_SPACE_MAX : u64 = 256

  // _HID values, as the integer EISAID a DSDT actually compiles to, for
  // the two PCI host-bridge identifiers. PNP0A03 is the conventional PCI
  // host bridge; PNP0A08 is the PCI Express one. See
  // aml_region_pci_context for why a POSITIVELY IDENTIFIED host bridge is
  // the only place a bus number may be defaulted.
  pub let AML_EISAID_PNP0A03 : u64 = 0x030AD041
  pub let AML_EISAID_PNP0A08 : u64 = 0x080AD041

  // ==========================================================================
  // Storage — CONFINED TO THIS OBJECT (see the header, property 3).
  //
  // Row layout, `aml_region_tab[b*8 + k]`:
  //   [0] node        OPREGION arena node; 0 = free row
  //   [1] space       RegionSpaceKeyword
  //   [2] decl_base   the address the TABLE asked for. Recorded for
  //                   reporting only; never used to form an address.
  //   [3] decl_len    length in bytes; the clip every access uses
  //   [4] host_base   host address of decl_base. THE ONLY ADDRESS THE
  //                   ACCESS STEP TOUCHES.
  //   [5] cap_handle  non-zero token of the KIND_OP_REGION the window
  //                   came from. Zero is impossible in a live row.
  //   [6] win_base    the capability window's base
  //   [7] win_len     the capability window's length
  //
  // aml_region_state: [0] live bindings, [1] unit accesses, [2] refusals.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut aml_region_tab   : [u64; 128] = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut aml_region_state : [u64; 8]   = uninit @align(8)

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_reset() — drop every binding.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): zero all 16 rows and the state block. Bulk-zeroing here rather than lazily is not tidiness: a stale row is a recorded permission to touch a host address, and a fresh session that found one would access memory the previous session's capability justified. Row 0 is zeroed with the rest and stays the null sentinel. Reserved labels: amr_rs_.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor r8, r8;
      amr_rs_loop:
        cmp r8, 128;
        jae amr_rs_state;
        mov [rcx], rax;
        add rcx, 8;
        add r8, 1;
        jmp amr_rs_loop;
      amr_rs_state:
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov [rcx + 0], rax;
        mov [rcx + 8], rax;
        mov [rcx + 16], rax;
        mov [rcx + 24], rax;
        mov [rcx + 32], rax;
        mov [rcx + 40], rax;
        mov [rcx + 48], rax;
        mov [rcx + 56], rax;

        // The EC gate is deliberately NOT fully cleared. Word 0 is the
        // registered backing — a system fact about whether R31's driver
        // exists, not a per-session one, and clearing it here would
        // deregister the EC driver on every table evaluation. Word 1 is
        // the committed-transaction count, left standing so
        // "this process has never performed an EC transaction" is a
        // whole-process claim. Only word 2, this session's gated
        // refusals, is reset.
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_ec_state];
        mov [rcx + 16], rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_count : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): number of live bindings. Leaf accessor. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 0];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_region_accesses : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): total unit accesses performed since reset. Exists so the corpus can assert the fuel spend and the access count agree -- a handler that spent fuel once per FIELD rather than once per ACCESS UNIT would pass every value assertion and still under-price a wide unaligned field. Leaf accessor. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 8];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_region_refusals : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): count of binds refused. A refusal is a security event -- a table asked for a window nobody granted -- and counting it separately from the latched error code means a fixture can prove a refusal HAPPENED even when a later success overwrites the latch. Leaf accessor. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 16];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_space_supported(space) -> u64
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_space_supported : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (space: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062), extended by #1063 / #1064 / #1065: 1 iff this handler services the space. Four rows: SystemMemory (0), SystemIO (1), PCI_Config (2), EmbeddedControl (3). THE REMAINING SPACES ARE STILL REFUSED WITH AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE (62), and the list is worth naming because the refusal is now narrow enough to enumerate: SMBus (4), SystemCMOS (5), PciBarTarget (6), IPMI (7), GeneralPurposeIO (8), GenericSerialBus (9), PCC (10), and every vendor-defined space at 0x80 and above. Each of those is a BUS PROTOCOL, not a flat window -- an access is a transaction with a command byte and a reply, not a load from an offset -- so servicing them as memory or as ports would run a protocol field as a raw address. SystemCMOS deserves a specific note because it is the one that LOOKS like it could ride the SystemIO path: it is reached through ports 0x70/0x71, but it is an INDEX/DATA pair, so offset N is not port 0x70+N and treating it as such would write the index register with data. It stays refused. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_sp_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;                      // AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_MEMORY
        je amr_sp_yes;
        cmp rdi, 1;                      // AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_IO
        je amr_sp_yes;
        cmp rdi, 2;                      // AML_SPACE_PCI_CONFIG
        je amr_sp_yes;
        cmp rdi, 3;                      // AML_SPACE_EC
        je amr_sp_yes;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      amr_sp_yes:
        mov rax, 1;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_mask(n) -> u64 — low-n-bits mask, correct at n == 64.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_mask : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (n: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): (1 << n) - 1 for n in 0..64. The n >= 64 case is handled explicitly and is not pedantry: x86 masks the shift count to 6 bits, so `shl rax, 64` is a NO-OP and the naive form yields 1-1 = 0 -- a full-width field would then read and write ZERO bits, and the failure is silent because every value it produces is a plausible 0. n == 0 yields 0, which is the right answer and is never reached by the field loops (they compute n >= 1 by construction). Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_mk_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 64;
        jae amr_mk_all;
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je amr_mk_none;
        mov rcx, rdi;
        mov rax, 1;
        shl rax, cl;
        sub rax, 1;
        ret;
      amr_mk_all:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        ret;
      amr_mk_none:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_contains(w_base, w_len, d_base, d_len) -> u64
  //
  // Overflow-safe containment — the userspace mirror of the kernel's
  // opregion_range_contains, and deliberately the same shape.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_contains : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (w_base: u64) (w_len: u64) (d_base: u64) (d_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): 1 iff [d_base, d_base+d_len) is inside [w_base, w_base+w_len). NOT ONE COMPARISON ADDS TWO CALLER-SUPPLIED VALUES, because d_base and d_len both come from a firmware table and `d_base + d_len` wraps for d_base = 2^64-1, d_len = 2 -- reporting CONTAINED for a region that starts at the top of the address space and runs off the end of it. Gates: w_len == 0 -> 0; d_len == 0 -> 0 (a zero-length region is not a region, and admitting one would let a table register an arbitrary address and read it back out of the binding, an address oracle needing no access); d_base < w_base -> 0; d_len > w_len -> 0 (checked before the subtraction below so it cannot underflow); (d_base - w_base) > (w_len - d_len) -> 0. Both subtractions are proven non-underflowing by the gates above them. This duplicates the kernel's opregion_range_contains deliberately: the two live on opposite sides of a privilege boundary and neither may depend on the other having run. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_ct_.",
      block: {
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je amr_ct_no;
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je amr_ct_no;
        cmp rdx, rdi;
        jb amr_ct_no;
        cmp rcx, rsi;
        ja amr_ct_no;

        mov r10, rdx;
        sub r10, rdi;                    // d_base - w_base
        mov r11, rsi;
        sub r11, rcx;                    // w_len - d_len
        cmp r10, r11;
        ja amr_ct_no;

        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      amr_ct_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // Row accessors. `b` is a binding index in 1..AML_REGION_MAX-1.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_row_live : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): 1 iff b names a live binding. THIS IS THE GATE EVERY ACCESS PATH OPENS WITH, and it asks three questions rather than one: b in 1..15 (index 0 is the null sentinel), node != 0, cap_handle != 0, host_base != 0. A row that failed any check in aml_region_bind was never written, so it fails here exactly as a free row does -- which is what makes 'the bind was refused' and 'there was never a bind' the same, safe outcome at the access site instead of two code paths one of which could be forgotten. Leaf; pure read. Reserved labels: amr_lv_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 1;
        jb amr_lv_no;
        cmp rdi, 16;                     // AML_REGION_MAX
        jae amr_lv_no;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 6;                       // b * 8 words * 8 bytes
        lea r9, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        add r8, r9;

        mov rax, [r8 + 0];               // node
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_lv_no;
        mov rax, [r8 + 40];              // cap_handle
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_lv_no;
        mov rax, [r8 + 32];              // host_base
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_lv_no;

        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      amr_lv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_row_word : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) (w: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): word w of row b, 0 for a dead row or an out-of-range word. The accessors below are written in terms of this one so the bounds check exists once. Leaf; pure read. Reserved labels: amr_rw_.",
      block: {
        cmp rsi, 8;
        jae amr_rw_no;
        cmp rdi, 1;
        jb amr_rw_no;
        cmp rdi, 16;
        jae amr_rw_no;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 6;
        mov r9, rsi;
        shl r9, 3;
        add r8, r9;
        lea r9, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        add r8, r9;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        ret;

      amr_rw_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_node : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the OPREGION arena node a binding covers. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        xor rsi, rsi;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_space : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062), narrowed by #1064: the address space of a binding. Word 1 now carries the space in bits [7:0] and, for a PCI_Config binding, the resolved seg/bus/dev/func context in bits [39:8] -- so THIS ACCESSOR MUST MASK. Returning the raw word was correct while every row's word 1 was a bare space id; it would now return an enormous number for any PCI binding, and every space comparison in the module goes through here. The mask is the reason the packing is safe. Reserved labels: amr_sc_.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 1;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        and rax, 0xFF;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_pci_ctx(b) -> (seg << 16) | (bus << 8) | (dev << 3) | func
  //
  // Zero for a binding that is not PCI_Config. Zero is NOT ambiguous with
  // "segment 0, bus 0, device 0, function 0" here, because a PCI_Config
  // row is only ever written after aml_region_pci_context RESOLVED a
  // context, and a resolved context for 0000:00:00.0 still sets the space
  // to 2 — the caller asks aml_region_space first. See #1064's note in
  // the header: the one thing this module must never do is behave as
  // though an unresolved context were 0000:00:00.0.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_pci_ctx : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-004 (#1064): the resolved PCI context packed into word 1 bits [39:8]. Exposed so the corpus can assert WHICH function a binding resolved to -- the failure this issue exists to prevent is addressing the wrong device, and that failure is invisible unless the resolved context is observable. A shifted read of the same word aml_region_space masks. Reserved labels: amr_pc_.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 1;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        shr rax, 8;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_base : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the DECLARED base of a binding -- the address the firmware table asked for. Exposed for reporting and for the corpus; the access step never reads it, which is the point. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 2;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_len : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the declared length of a binding, in bytes. This is the clip every access is measured against. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 3;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_cap : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the capability handle the binding was derived from. Non-zero in every live row by construction. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rsi, 5;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_find(node) -> b | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_find : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the binding index for an OPREGION node, 0 if unbound. Linear scan over 15 rows; the table is small and the alternative -- caching the index in the arena node -- would mutate the parse tree, which #1057 established must stay byte-identical across an evaluation so it remains valid as the IPC wire format. A zero node argument answers 0 rather than matching the free rows, which is what stops a caller that lost track of its node from binding onto the first free slot. Leaf; pure read. Reserved labels: amr_fd_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je amr_fd_no;

        mov r8, 1;
      amr_fd_loop:
        cmp r8, 16;
        jae amr_fd_no;

        mov r9, r8;
        shl r9, 6;
        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        add r9, r10;
        mov rax, [r9 + 0];
        cmp rax, rdi;
        je amr_fd_hit;
        add r8, 1;
        jmp amr_fd_loop;

      amr_fd_hit:
        mov rax, r8;
        ret;

      amr_fd_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // R30.M3-003 (#1063) — THE PORT TRANSACTION.
  //
  // These two functions are the ONLY places in this repository that
  // contain an `in` or an `out` instruction. That is the whole of the
  // transaction/logic split for SystemIO: every other line of the port
  // path — the 16-bit bound, the width resolution, the bit-granular field
  // walk, the update rule, the fuel accounting — is the SAME CODE the
  // SystemMemory path runs, and is exercised by the corpus against a
  // synthetic buffer.
  //
  // WHY THE CORPUS CANNOT FAKE A PORT ACCESS, AND WHY THAT IS THE POINT.
  // The corpus runs these objects in ring 3 under Linux with IOPL 0. An
  // `in` or `out` executed there raises #GP, which arrives as SIGSEGV.
  // So the corpus cannot accidentally "pass" by performing a real port
  // access, and it cannot simulate one either. It does two things
  // instead, and together they cover both halves:
  //
  //   * With a synthetic backing (host_va = a mapped buffer) it runs the
  //     entire logic path for real and asserts on the bytes.
  //   * With host_va = AML_REGION_IO_REAL it asserts, under the
  //     harness's SIGSEGV guard, that the access TRAPS — which is
  //     positive evidence that the sentinel path reaches a genuine port
  //     instruction and not a memory load. A mutant that serviced the
  //     real sentinel as a memory access would NOT trap, and dies there.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_port_in : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (port: u64) (nbytes: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-003 (#1063): read 1, 2 or 4 bytes from an I/O port. The port goes to DX because that is the only register form of the instruction that reaches the whole 16-bit port space -- the immediate form covers ports 0..255 only, and a handler restricted to those would silently fail on every LPC device above 0x100. THERE IS NO 8-BYTE FORM, and its absence here is load-bearing: nbytes == 8 falls through to the zero return rather than being emulated as two dword reads, because two reads of a port are two TRANSACTIONS, and a port that pops a FIFO would have popped it twice. The caller refuses QWordAcc on SystemIO before ever reaching this function; this is the second line of that same defence. Each narrow form is preceded by an explicit xor of rax: `in al, dx` writes only AL and `in ax, dx` only AX, leaving the upper bits of rax as whatever the port computation left there -- a byte port would otherwise read back as a plausible enormous number. Leaf; no calls, so no frame arithmetic. Reserved labels: amr_pi_.",
      block: {
        mov rdx, rdi;                    // port -> DX
        cmp rsi, 1;
        je amr_pi_b;
        cmp rsi, 2;
        je amr_pi_w;
        cmp rsi, 4;
        je amr_pi_d;
        xor rax, rax;                    // no 8-byte port access exists
        ret;
      amr_pi_b:
        xor rax, rax;
        in_al rax;
        ret;
      amr_pi_w:
        xor rax, rax;
        in_ax rax;
        ret;
      amr_pi_d:
        xor rax, rax;
        in_eax rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let aml_region_port_out : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (port: u64) (nbytes: u64) (value: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-003 (#1063): write 1, 2 or 4 bytes to an I/O port; returns 1 on success and 0 for an unsupported width. The value arrives in RDX and the port must GO to RDX, so the value is parked in r10 before the port is moved in -- getting that order wrong would write the port number to the port, which is the kind of bug that looks like working code on any port whose value happens to be harmless. Returns 0 rather than approximating for nbytes == 8, for the same reason the read does. Leaf; no calls. Reserved labels: amr_po_.",
      block: {
        mov r10, rdx;                    // value, before RDX is overwritten
        mov rdx, rdi;                    // port -> DX
        mov rax, r10;                    // value -> AL / AX / EAX
        cmp rsi, 1;
        je amr_po_b;
        cmp rsi, 2;
        je amr_po_w;
        cmp rsi, 4;
        je amr_po_d;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      amr_po_b:
        out_al rax;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      amr_po_w:
        out_ax rax;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      amr_po_d:
        out_eax rax;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // R30.M3-005 (#1065) — THE EMBEDDED CONTROLLER GATE.
  //
  // The EC driver is R31 and DOES NOT EXIST. This milestone lands the
  // handler contract, the address-space registration and the bind-time
  // validation, and gates the transaction itself — the same shape iter 32
  // used to gate VT-d programming on R22.M5.
  //
  // WHAT AN EC ACCESS ACTUALLY IS, so the R31 landing has a spec and not
  // a guess. It is not a load. Reading one byte of EC space is a
  // handshake over two ports (ACPI 6.5 §12.2): write the RD_EC command
  // (0x80) to the command port (conventionally 0x66), poll the status
  // register until IBF clears, write the address byte to the data port
  // (conventionally 0x62), poll until OBF sets, read the data byte.
  // A write is WR_EC (0x81) with an extra address/data phase. Every step
  // can time out; a real EC takes milliseconds and some take longer under
  // load. THE PORTS ARE NOT FIXED — they come from the EC device's
  // _CRS, and assuming 0x62/0x66 is wrong on machines that relocate them.
  //
  // WHAT R31 MUST ADD:
  //   1. A driver that owns the two ports (from _CRS, not hardcoded) and
  //      serialises every transaction — the EC has ONE outstanding
  //      transaction, and interleaving two corrupts both.
  //   2. Timeout and recovery, because a wedged EC must not wedge the
  //      interpreter. This is why the access cannot simply be inlined
  //      here: it needs to block and to fail, and the AML evaluator's
  //      fuel model has no notion of waiting.
  //   3. _GPE query handling (KIND_EC_QUERY): the EC raises an SCI, the
  //      driver issues QR_EC (0x84), and the returned byte selects a _Qxx
  //      method to run. That is a REENTRY into the interpreter and needs
  //      the serialisation story settled first.
  //   4. A call to aml_region_ec_backing_set with its transaction entry
  //      point, which is what opens this gate.
  //
  // WHY NOTHING IS SIMULATED. A handler that returned plausible bytes
  // would make thermal, battery and lid code appear to work while reading
  // fiction — a battery gauge that reads 100% from a fabricated EC is
  // worse than one that reports an error, because nothing downstream can
  // tell. Every EC transaction therefore returns AML_ERR_REGION_EC_GATED
  // (67) and produces NO VALUE.
  //
  // THE FLIP ASSERTION. aml_region_ec_hw_committed() counts transactions
  // a registered backing actually performed. It is 0, and the corpus
  // asserts it is 0. When R31 registers a backing and a transaction runs,
  // that assertion fails and must be rewritten — which is the point: the
  // gate cannot be quietly left closed after the hardware lands, and it
  // cannot be quietly opened before.
  //
  // aml_region_ec_state: [0] registered backing entry (0 = gated),
  //                      [1] transactions committed by the backing,
  //                      [2] gated refusals this session.
  //
  // [0] and [1] deliberately SURVIVE aml_region_reset. Registration is a
  // system fact — R31's driver is present or it is not — and clearing it
  // per AML session would deregister the EC driver on every table
  // evaluation. [1] survives so "this process has never performed an EC
  // transaction" is a whole-process claim rather than a per-session one.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut aml_region_ec_state : [u64; 4] = uninit @align(8)

  pub let aml_region_ec_backing_set : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (entry: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-005 (#1065): THE REGISTRATION POINT R31 CALLS, and the one function in this module that nothing calls today. It exists now, rather than being added with R31, so that the gate has a named opening and the shape of what R31 must supply is fixed by this milestone instead of negotiated later. tools/verify-aml-parser.sh asserts that NO object relocates against this symbol -- an assertion that is true precisely because the EC driver does not exist, and that R31 must update when it does. That is a stronger statement than a comment: it makes 'the gate is still closed' a build-time fact. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_ec_state];
        mov [rcx + 0], rdi;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_region_ec_backing : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-005 (#1065): the registered EC transaction entry, 0 while the gate is closed. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_ec_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 0];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_region_ec_hw_committed : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-005 (#1065): EC transactions a registered backing has actually performed. THE FLIP ASSERTION READS THIS. It is 0 today and the corpus pins it at 0; when R31 lands and an EC transaction runs, the pin fails and must be rewritten deliberately. Not cleared by aml_region_reset, so the claim it supports is 'this process has never touched an EC', not 'not since the last reset'. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_ec_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 8];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let aml_region_ec_gated : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-005 (#1065): EC accesses refused by the closed gate this session. Counted separately from the latched error code so a fixture can prove the refusal HAPPENED even when a later success overwrites the latch -- the same device aml_region_refusals uses for the bind path. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        lea rcx, [rip + aml_region_ec_state];
        mov rax, [rcx + 16];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // R30.M3-004 (#1064) — RESOLVING THE PCI DEVICE CONTEXT.
  //
  // A PCI_Config OperationRegion does NOT carry a bus, device or
  // function. Its declared "offset" is an offset into the configuration
  // space of the function it is DECLARED INSIDE, and that function is
  // named by the enclosing Device's _ADR, on the bus named by the
  // enclosing host bridge's _BBN in the segment named by its _SEG.
  //
  // GETTING THIS WRONG IS NOT AN ERROR, IT IS SILENT CORRUPTION. An
  // OpRegion that resolves to the wrong function reads and writes another
  // device's configuration space — its BARs, its command register, its
  // bus-master enable — while every bounds check in this module passes
  // and every value returned looks entirely plausible. That is why the
  // rule here is REFUSE, NEVER DEFAULT.
  //
  // WHERE THE ONE PERMITTED DEFAULT LIVES. _BBN and _SEG are genuinely
  // optional, and ACPI says a host bridge without them is segment 0 bus
  // 0. That default is sanctioned ONLY for a host bridge, so it is
  // applied only after one has been POSITIVELY IDENTIFIED by a _HID of
  // PNP0A03 or PNP0A08. A Device with an _ADR and no host bridge anywhere
  // in its ancestry is not "probably on bus 0" — it is not a PCI device
  // at all, or the table is malformed, and either way the answer is
  // AML_ERR_REGION_PCI_CONTEXT (66).
  //
  // THE ECAM ADDRESS IS NOT COMPUTED HERE, AND THAT IS THE R22 REUSE.
  // This module never learns an ECAM base. It resolves a {seg, bus, dev,
  // func} and the SUPERVISOR asks the kernel for an OpRegion capability
  // over that function's configuration space; the kernel's
  // src/kernel/core/pci/config.pdx resolves the segment base through
  // mcfg_ecam_base_for_segment (R20-M3-001) and adds the same
  // (bus << 20) | (dev << 15) | (func << 12) offset R22 has always used.
  // The window that comes back is a memory window, which is why
  // opregion_space_base_kind maps PCI_Config to KIND_MEMORY, and why the
  // ACCESS half of this handler is the memory path unchanged. There is no
  // second ECAM implementation, and userspace cannot construct one
  // because it never sees a segment base.
  //
  // aml_region_pci_ecam_offset mirrors the OFFSET arithmetic only, so the
  // corpus can assert the two agree without this module holding an
  // address.
  //
  // CONTEXT PACKING:
  //   bit  40     VALID — set on every resolved context
  //   bits [31:16] segment
  //   bits [15:8]  bus
  //   bits [7:3]   device
  //   bits [2:0]   function
  //
  // The VALID bit exists because 0000:00:00.0 is a REAL function — it is
  // typically the host bridge itself — so a packed context of all zeroes
  // must not be confusable with "unresolved". Without the bit, the exact
  // failure this issue exists to prevent would be spelled `0`.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let AML_PCI_CTX_VALID : u64 = 0x10000000000

  pub let AML_SEG_ADR : u64 = 0x5244415F     // '_ADR'
  pub let AML_SEG_HID : u64 = 0x4449485F     // '_HID'
  pub let AML_SEG_BBN : u64 = 0x4E42425F     // '_BBN'
  pub let AML_SEG_SEG : u64 = 0x4745535F     // '_SEG'

  pub let aml_region_pci_ecam_offset : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (ctx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-004 (#1064): the ECAM byte offset of a packed context's function, (bus << 20) | (dev << 15) | (func << 12) -- byte-for-byte the arithmetic pci_config_read_u32 performs in src/kernel/core/pci/config.pdx. Reproduced here ONLY so the corpus can assert the two agree; nothing in this module adds it to a base, because this module never holds one. If R22's layout ever changed, the corpus assertion that pins these together is what would catch this copy drifting. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_eo_.",
      block: {
        mov r10, rdi;
        shr r10, 8;
        and r10, 0xFF;                   // bus
        shl r10, 20;

        mov r11, rdi;
        shr r11, 3;
        and r11, 0x1F;                   // dev
        shl r11, 15;
        add r10, r11;

        mov r11, rdi;
        and r11, 0x07;                   // func
        shl r11, 12;
        add r10, r11;

        mov rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_named_int(scope, seg) -> NAME node | 0
  //
  // The NAME node is returned rather than its value because a value of 0
  // is legitimate for every one of _ADR, _BBN and _SEG, so "absent" and
  // "zero" must be distinguishable at the call site.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_named_int : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (scope: u64) (seg: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-004 (#1064): the direct child of `scope` that is a NAME node (kind 5) holding an INTEGER (data kind 1) whose single name segment is `seg`; 0 if there is none. Only a DIRECT child, and only a Name: an _ADR that is a Method is NOT evaluated here, and returns 0 so the caller refuses. That is deliberate -- evaluating a method to resolve a region binding would run vendor AML at bind time, in a context where the region it is binding is not yet available, and the reentrancy is exactly the sort of thing a hostile table would aim for. Real DSDTs write _ADR, _BBN and _SEG as constant Names; the ones that do not are refused rather than guessed. The segcount == 1 test rejects a multi-segment path that happens to end in the right four characters. The walk is BOUNDED at 4096 siblings (the arena's own capacity) so a corrupted next_sibling cycle terminates instead of hanging the supervisor. r12=wanted seg, r13=cursor, r14=name_ref, r15=step budget. Reserved labels: amr_ni_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rsi;                    // wanted segment
        mov r15, 4096;                   // step budget

        call aml_node_first_child;
        mov r13, rax;

      amr_ni_loop:
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_ni_none;
        cmp r15, 0;
        je amr_ni_none;
        sub r15, 1;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 5;                      // AML_NODE_NAME
        jne amr_ni_next;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_flags;
        cmp rax, 1;                      // data kind 1 = integer
        jne amr_ni_next;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_name;
        mov r14, rax;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_ni_next;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_name_segcount;
        cmp rax, 1;
        jne amr_ni_next;

        mov rdi, r14;
        xor rsi, rsi;
        call aml_name_seg;
        cmp rax, r12;
        je amr_ni_found;

      amr_ni_next:
        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_next_sibling;
        mov r13, rax;
        jmp amr_ni_loop;

      amr_ni_found:
        mov rax, r13;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_ni_none:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_named_int_val(name_node) -> value
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_named_int_val : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (name_node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-004 (#1064): the integer a NAME node holds, via its arg0 index into the u64 side table. Split from the lookup so the lookup can answer 'absent' with 0 while this answers 'the value is 0' with the same 0 -- the caller has already distinguished them by checking the node. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        call aml_node_arg0;
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_u64_get;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_enclosing_device(node) -> DEVICE node | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_enclosing_device : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-004 (#1064): the nearest DEVICE (kind 3) strictly above `node`, or 0. The walk STOPS AT ROOT (kind 1) rather than treating the root as a device -- an OperationRegion declared at table scope has no _ADR to inherit, and answering 'the root' would put the caller one step from defaulting to 00:00.0. Bounded at 256 levels: a namespace that deep is already malformed, and an unbounded parent walk over a corrupted arena would not terminate. r12=cursor, r13=budget. Reserved labels: amr_ed_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, 256;

      amr_ed_loop:
        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_ed_none;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_ed_none;
        sub r13, 1;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_parent;
        mov r12, rax;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_ed_none;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 3;                      // AML_NODE_DEVICE
        je amr_ed_found;
        cmp rax, 1;                      // AML_NODE_ROOT
        je amr_ed_none;
        jmp amr_ed_loop;

      amr_ed_found:
        mov rax, r12;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_ed_none:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_host_bridge(dev) -> DEVICE node | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_host_bridge : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (dev: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-004 (#1064): the nearest enclosing PCI host bridge at or above `dev`, identified by a _HID of PNP0A03 (0x030AD041) or PNP0A08 (0x080AD041); 0 if there is none. THE SEARCH STARTS AT `dev` ITSELF because an OperationRegion may be declared directly inside the host bridge device. A _HID written as a STRING rather than an EisaId integer is NOT matched and therefore causes a refusal upstream: the string form is legal ACPI, but reading it requires the AML buffer, which is confined to aml_lex.o, and guessing that an unreadable _HID is probably a host bridge is exactly the defaulting this issue forbids. Refusing is the honest boundary and is recorded as such. Bounded at 256 levels. r12=cursor, r13=budget, r14=_HID node. Reserved labels: amr_hb_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, 256;

      amr_hb_loop:
        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_hb_none;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_hb_none;
        sub r13, 1;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 3;                      // DEVICE
        jne amr_hb_up;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, 0x4449485F;             // '_HID'
        call aml_region_named_int;
        mov r14, rax;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_hb_up;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_region_named_int_val;
        mov r11, 0x030AD041;             // PNP0A03
        cmp rax, r11;
        je amr_hb_found;
        mov r11, 0x080AD041;             // PNP0A08
        cmp rax, r11;
        je amr_hb_found;

      amr_hb_up:
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 1;                      // ROOT — stop, do not default
        je amr_hb_none;
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_parent;
        mov r12, rax;
        jmp amr_hb_loop;

      amr_hb_found:
        mov rax, r12;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_hb_none:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_pci_context(opregion_node) -> packed ctx | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_pci_context : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-004 (#1064): resolve the {segment, bus, device, function} a PCI_Config OperationRegion belongs to, or 0 with AML_ERR_REGION_PCI_CONTEXT (66) latched. EVERY FAILURE PATH REFUSES; NOT ONE OF THEM SUBSTITUTES A DEFAULT. Gates: (1) node is a live OPREGION. (2) an enclosing Device exists. (3) that Device has a constant integer _ADR. (4) _ADR fits in 32 bits, its high word (the device number) is <= 31 and its low word (the function) is <= 7 -- a malformed _ADR is refused rather than MASKED, because masking 0x00200003 down to device 0 would address a completely different function while looking successful, which is this issue's headline hazard. (5) a host bridge is positively identified above it. Only THEN are the spec-sanctioned defaults applied: _BBN absent means bus 0 and _SEG absent means segment 0, and both are legal only because step 5 proved we are standing on a host bridge. A present-but-out-of-range _BBN (> 255) or _SEG (> 0xFFFF) is refused, not truncated. The result carries AML_PCI_CTX_VALID because 0000:00:00.0 is a real function and must not pack to the same value as 'unresolved'. r12=opregion, r13=device, r14=host bridge, r15=accumulating context. Reserved labels: amr_px_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 16;

        mov r12, rdi;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_px_no;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 10;                     // AML_NODE_OPREGION
        jne amr_px_no;

        // -- Gate 2: the enclosing Device.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_enclosing_device;
        mov r13, rax;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_px_no;

        // -- Gate 3: a constant integer _ADR on it.
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, 0x5244415F;             // '_ADR'
        call aml_region_named_int;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_px_no;

        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_region_named_int_val;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;              // _ADR

        // -- Gate 4: _ADR is a well-formed 32-bit device/function pair.
        mov r10, rax;
        shr r10, 32;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne amr_px_no;

        mov r10, rax;
        shr r10, 16;                     // device
        cmp r10, 31;
        ja amr_px_no;

        mov r11, rax;
        and r11, 0xFFFF;                 // function
        cmp r11, 7;
        ja amr_px_no;

        // ctx so far: (dev << 3) | func
        shl r10, 3;
        add r10, r11;
        mov r15, r10;

        // -- Gate 5: a POSITIVELY IDENTIFIED host bridge.
        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_region_host_bridge;
        mov r14, rax;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_px_no;

        // -- _BBN, defaulting to 0 only now.
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, 0x4E42425F;             // '_BBN'
        call aml_region_named_int;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_px_seg;
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_region_named_int_val;
        cmp rax, 255;
        ja amr_px_no;
        shl rax, 8;
        add r15, rax;

      amr_px_seg:
        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, 0x4745535F;             // '_SEG'
        call aml_region_named_int;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_px_ok;
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_region_named_int_val;
        mov r11, 0xFFFF;
        cmp rax, r11;
        ja amr_px_no;
        shl rax, 16;
        add r15, rax;

      amr_px_ok:
        mov r11, 0x10000000000;          // AML_PCI_CTX_VALID
        add r15, r11;
        mov rax, r15;
        add rsp, 16;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_px_no:
        mov rdi, 66;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_PCI_CONTEXT
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        xor rax, rax;
        add rsp, 16;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_bind(node, cap_handle, win_base, win_len, host_va) -> b | 0
  //
  // THE MAPPING STEP. The only writer of a row, and the only place a
  // host address enters this module.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_bind : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) (cap_handle: u64) (win_base: u64)
       (win_len: u64) (host_va: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): bind a firmware-declared OperationRegion to a capability window. THE DECLARED RANGE COMES FROM THE PARSE TREE (the untrusted side: node arg0/arg1 index the u64 side table) AND THE WINDOW COMES FROM THE CAPABILITY (the trusted side, read out of the KIND_OP_REGION row by the caller) -- the two are never the same argument, which is what makes the containment check meaningful rather than a comparison of a value with itself. Gates in order: (1) node is a live OPREGION (kind 10) else AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND. (2) cap_handle == 0, win_len == 0 or host_va == 0 -> AML_ERR_REGION_NO_CAP. THIS IS THE HEADLINE REFUSAL: a region the supervisor holds no capability for is refused before any address is formed, and the refusal counter is bumped so a fixture can prove it happened. (3) space unsupported -> AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE. (4) declared length zero -> AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED. (5) aml_region_contains(win_base, win_len, decl_base, decl_len) == 0 -> AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED, WITH NO ROW WRITTEN AND NO CLIPPING -- clipping would turn the refusal into an address oracle. (6) already bound -> return the existing index rather than a second row, so a re-bind cannot produce two rows for one region with different windows. (7) free row scan from index 1 (0 is the null sentinel); exhaustion -> AML_ERR_REGION_TABLE_FULL. host_base = host_va + (decl_base - win_base), the one address the access step will ever use; the subtraction cannot underflow because gate 5 proved decl_base >= win_base. Registers r12=node, r13=cap_handle, r14=decl_base, r15=decl_len; win_base/win_len/host_va spilled. Reserved labels: amr_bd_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 48;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // node
        mov r13, rsi;                    // cap_handle
        mov [rsp + 0], rdx;              // win_base
        mov [rsp + 8], rcx;              // win_len
        mov [rsp + 16], r8;              // host_va

        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_bd_unbound;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 10;                     // AML_NODE_OPREGION
        jne amr_bd_unbound;

        // -- Gate 2: is there a capability at all?
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_bd_no_cap;
        mov rax, [rsp + 8];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_no_cap;
        mov rax, [rsp + 16];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_no_cap;

        // -- Gate 3: space.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_flags;
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;             // space
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_region_space_supported;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_bad_space;

        // -- Declared base / length, out of the u64 side table.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg0;
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_u64_get;
        mov r14, rax;                    // decl_base

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg1;
        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_u64_get;
        mov r15, rax;                    // decl_len

        cmp r15, 0;
        je amr_bd_not_cov;

        // -- Gate 4b: SPACE-SPECIFIC VALIDATION (#1063 / #1064 / #1065).
        //
        // These run BEFORE the containment check, because they ask a
        // question the capability cannot answer: whether the declared
        // range is addressable AT ALL in this space, and — for PCI — WHICH
        // FUNCTION it names. A port above 0xFFFF is not merely
        // unauthorised, and a PCI region whose device context cannot be
        // resolved must be refused whether or not some window covers it.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rsp + 32], rax;             // packed PCI context; 0 unless resolved

        // The real-port sentinel is meaningless outside SystemIO. Admitting
        // it elsewhere would store host_base = 1 and send the memory path
        // dereferencing the zero page.
        mov rax, [rsp + 16];
        cmp rax, 1;                      // AML_REGION_IO_REAL
        jne amr_bd_sent_ok;
        mov rax, [rsp + 24];
        cmp rax, 1;                      // AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_IO
        jne amr_bd_no_cap;
      amr_bd_sent_ok:

        mov rax, [rsp + 24];             // space
        cmp rax, 1;
        je amr_bd_io_range;
        cmp rax, 2;
        je amr_bd_pci;
        cmp rax, 3;
        je amr_bd_ec_range;
        jmp amr_bd_range_ok;

        // SystemIO: the port space is 16 bits. Overflow-safe, in the same
        // shape as aml_region_contains: decl_len is compared against the
        // whole space first so the subtraction below cannot underflow, and
        // `decl_base + decl_len` is never formed.
      amr_bd_io_range:
        mov r10, 0x10000;
        cmp r15, r10;
        ja amr_bd_port_range;
        sub r10, r15;
        cmp r14, r10;
        ja amr_bd_port_range;
        jmp amr_bd_range_ok;

        // EmbeddedControl: 256 bytes of EC RAM.
      amr_bd_ec_range:
        mov r10, 256;                    // AML_EC_SPACE_MAX
        cmp r15, r10;
        ja amr_bd_not_cov;
        sub r10, r15;
        cmp r14, r10;
        ja amr_bd_not_cov;
        jmp amr_bd_range_ok;

        // PCI_Config: bounded by extended config space, then the device
        // context RESOLVED — never defaulted.
      amr_bd_pci:
        mov r10, 4096;                   // AML_PCI_CFG_EXT
        cmp r15, r10;
        ja amr_bd_not_cov;
        sub r10, r15;
        cmp r14, r10;
        ja amr_bd_not_cov;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_pci_context;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_latched;               // PCI_CONTEXT already latched
        mov [rsp + 32], rax;

      amr_bd_range_ok:

        // -- Gate 5: CONTAINMENT.
        mov rdi, [rsp + 0];              // win_base
        mov rsi, [rsp + 8];              // win_len
        mov rdx, r14;                    // decl_base
        mov rcx, r15;                    // decl_len
        call aml_region_contains;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_not_cov;

        // -- Gate 6: already bound?
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_find;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne amr_bd_out;

        // -- Gate 7: free row.
        mov r8, 1;
      amr_bd_scan:
        cmp r8, 16;
        jae amr_bd_full;
        mov r9, r8;
        shl r9, 6;
        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_tab];
        add r9, r10;
        mov rax, [r9 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bd_found;
        add r8, 1;
        jmp amr_bd_scan;

      amr_bd_found:
        mov [r9 + 0], r12;               // node

        // Word 1 = space in [7:0], packed PCI context above it. The two
        // share a word rather than growing the row because the context is
        // meaningful for exactly one space, and a ninth word would be
        // unused in fifteen rows out of sixteen.
        mov rax, [rsp + 32];             // packed context (0 unless PCI)
        shl rax, 8;
        mov r11, [rsp + 24];             // space
        add rax, r11;
        mov [r9 + 8], rax;

        mov [r9 + 16], r14;              // decl_base
        mov [r9 + 24], r15;              // decl_len

        // host_base. For a real-port SystemIO binding the sentinel is
        // stored VERBATIM: there is no mapping to offset into, and adding
        // the window offset to it would turn 1 into a small integer that
        // the access step could no longer recognise as the sentinel — and
        // would then dereference. For every other binding it is
        // host_va + (decl_base - win_base), the one address the access
        // step will ever use.
        mov rax, [rsp + 16];             // host_va
        cmp rax, 1;                      // AML_REGION_IO_REAL
        je amr_bd_hb_done;
        mov rax, r14;
        mov r10, [rsp + 0];
        sub rax, r10;                    // offset into the window
        mov r10, [rsp + 16];
        add rax, r10;
      amr_bd_hb_done:
        mov [r9 + 32], rax;              // host_base

        mov [r9 + 40], r13;              // cap_handle
        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        mov [r9 + 48], rax;              // win_base
        mov rax, [rsp + 8];
        mov [r9 + 56], rax;              // win_len

        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;              // live bindings

        mov rax, r8;                     // binding index
        jmp amr_bd_out;

      amr_bd_no_cap:
        mov rdi, 56;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_NO_CAP
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_not_cov:
        mov rdi, 57;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_NOT_COVERED
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_bad_space:
        mov rdi, 62;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_SPACE
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_full:
        mov rdi, 63;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_TABLE_FULL
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_port_range:
        mov rdi, 65;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_PORT_RANGE
        jmp amr_bd_refuse;
      amr_bd_unbound:
        mov rdi, 58;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND

      amr_bd_refuse:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        // A refusal whose code was ALREADY latched by a callee enters
        // here, so the refusal counter moves exactly once per refused
        // bind and the callee's more specific code is not overwritten by
        // a generic one. aml_region_pci_context is the only such callee
        // today: it knows which of five distinct context failures
        // occurred, and this path preserves that.
      amr_bd_latched:
        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 16], rax;             // refusals
        xor rax, rax;

      amr_bd_out:
        add rsp, 48;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_bounds_ok(b, off, nbytes) -> u64
  //
  // THE CLIP. An access unit must lie entirely inside the region.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_bounds_ok : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) (off: u64) (nbytes: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): 1 iff [off, off+nbytes) lies entirely inside the binding's declared length; otherwise 0, having latched AML_ERR_REGION_OOB. Overflow-safe: len < nbytes -> refuse, then off > len - nbytes -> refuse, so `off + nbytes` is never formed and an off near 2^64 cannot wrap into range. A STRADDLING ACCESS IS REFUSED, NOT NARROWED: the bytes that fit are not read, nothing is partially completed, and the declared transaction width is not silently reduced -- see the module header for why narrowing is the wrong repair. A dead binding is refused with AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND, so a caller cannot reach the address arithmetic with an index it never bound. Reserved labels: amr_bo_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, rsi;
        mov r14, rdx;

        call aml_region_row_live;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_bo_unbound;

        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_bo_oob;                   // a zero-width access is a bug

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_len;
        mov r15, rax;                    // decl_len

        cmp r15, r14;
        jb amr_bo_oob;                   // region shorter than one unit

        mov r10, r15;
        sub r10, r14;                    // len - nbytes
        cmp r13, r10;
        ja amr_bo_oob;

        mov rax, 1;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_bo_oob:
        mov rdi, 59;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_OOB
        jmp amr_bo_fail;
      amr_bo_unbound:
        mov rdi, 58;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND
      amr_bo_fail:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_read_unit(b, off, nbytes) -> value
  //
  // THE ACCESS STEP. Takes no address.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_read_unit : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) (off: u64) (nbytes: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): read one access unit of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes at byte offset `off` within binding `b`. Bounds are checked FIRST, before any address is formed, which is what lets the corpus place its synthetic backing buffer flush against a PROT_NONE guard page: a missing clip is a SIGSEGV, not a silently tolerated over-read of the next fixture. The address is host_base + off, and host_base is the value aml_region_bind computed from the CAPABILITY window -- the declared base is never added in here, so a table that lied about its address cannot steer the load. One fuel unit is spent per unit access via aml_eval_spend, so a field spanning K units costs K; the access counter moves with it. An nbytes that is not 1/2/4/8 is refused as AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH rather than approximated. Returns 0 on any refusal, with the code latched -- callers in the field loops call aml_region_bounds_ok first precisely because a legitimate unit value may also be 0. Reserved labels: amr_ru_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, rsi;
        mov r14, rdx;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r14;
        call aml_region_bounds_ok;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_ru_zero;

        // -- SPACE DISPATCH (#1063 / #1064 / #1065). The bounds check, the
        //    fuel spend and the access count are SHARED by every space and
        //    stay above the split: a per-space copy of any of them is a
        //    per-space chance to omit one.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_space;
        mov r15, rax;

        // EmbeddedControl is gated on R31 and refuses BEFORE the fuel
        // spend and BEFORE the access counter moves, because a gated
        // access performs no transaction and must not be counted as one.
        cmp r15, 3;                      // AML_SPACE_EC
        je amr_ru_ec;

        call aml_eval_spend;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_ru_zero;                  // fuel exhausted; already latched

        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        // SystemIO with the real-port sentinel is the only path that
        // leaves memory. Every other space — SystemMemory, PCI_Config
        // through its ECAM window, and SystemIO against a synthetic
        // backing — is the same sized load it always was.
        cmp r15, 1;                      // AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_IO
        jne amr_ru_mem;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, 4;                      // host_base
        call aml_region_row_word;
        cmp rax, 1;                      // AML_REGION_IO_REAL
        jne amr_ru_mem;

        // No 8-byte port transaction exists. Refused HERE, inside the
        // real-port branch, rather than left to aml_region_port_in's own
        // width guard: a caller reaching this width has already been let
        // through by the field layer's QWordAcc refusal, so it arrived by
        // a direct unit call, and it deserves the named error rather than
        // a bare 0. Placing it after the sentinel test also makes it the
        // corpus's discriminator -- a mutant that routed the sentinel to
        // the memory path would dereference address 1 instead of
        // returning this code.
        cmp r14, 8;
        je amr_ru_badw;

        // The port is decl_base + off. decl_base is used HERE, and only
        // here, as an address — which is sound for exactly the reason the
        // memory path refuses to do it: for port space the capability
        // window IS a port range, so the containment check at bind time
        // proved this port is one the supervisor holds.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_base;
        add rax, r13;                    // + off
        mov rdi, rax;
        mov rsi, r14;
        call aml_region_port_in;
        jmp amr_ru_out;

      amr_ru_mem:
        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, 4;                      // host_base
        call aml_region_row_word;
        add rax, r13;                    // + off
        mov r15, rax;                    // the one address

        cmp r14, 1;
        je amr_ru_b;
        cmp r14, 2;
        je amr_ru_w;
        cmp r14, 4;
        je amr_ru_d;
        cmp r14, 8;
        je amr_ru_q;

        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        jmp amr_ru_zero;

      amr_ru_badw:
        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        jmp amr_ru_zero;

        // R31 WIRES THE EC TRANSACTION IN AT THIS LABEL, dispatching
        // through aml_region_ec_backing() and incrementing
        // aml_region_ec_state[8] on each completed transaction. Until
        // then the refusal is unconditional and NO VALUE IS PRODUCED:
        // returning a plausible byte here would make thermal and battery
        // code appear to work while reading fiction.
      amr_ru_ec:
        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_ec_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 16], rax;             // gated refusals
        mov rdi, 67;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_EC_GATED
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        jmp amr_ru_zero;

        // The three narrow loads are each preceded by an explicit
        // zeroing of the destination. mov_b and mov_w write only the low
        // 8 / 16 bits of the register and LEAVE THE REST -- so without
        // this, a byte field would come back with whatever the address
        // computation left in the upper 56 bits, which is a plausible
        // enormous number rather than an obvious failure.
      amr_ru_b:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r15 + 0];
        jmp amr_ru_out;
      amr_ru_w:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_w rax, [r15 + 0];
        jmp amr_ru_out;
      amr_ru_d:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_d rax, [r15 + 0];
        jmp amr_ru_out;
      amr_ru_q:
        mov rax, [r15 + 0];
        jmp amr_ru_out;

      amr_ru_zero:
        xor rax, rax;

      amr_ru_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_write_unit(b, off, nbytes, value) -> 1 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_write_unit : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (b: u64) (off: u64) (nbytes: u64) (value: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): write one access unit of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes. Same clip-before-address discipline and the same single source of the address as the read. The store is SIZED (mov_b / mov_w / mov_d / plain mov): a 64-bit store used to write a byte field would clobber the seven following bytes, and those bytes are neighbouring hardware registers -- this is the single most consequential encoder choice in the file, which is why the widths are four explicit branches rather than a computed store. One fuel unit per unit access, matching the read. Reserved labels: amr_wu_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, rsi;
        mov r14, rdx;
        mov r15, rcx;                    // value

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, r13;
        mov rdx, r14;
        call aml_region_bounds_ok;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_wu_no;

        // -- SPACE DISPATCH, mirroring the read exactly. See there for why
        //    the bounds check, the fuel spend and the access count stay
        //    above the split.
        // All four callee-save registers already hold b / off / nbytes /
        // value, so the space is RE-READ rather than parked. It is a
        // bounds-checked load from a table this module owns, and paying
        // for it twice is cheaper than pushing a fifth register and
        // re-deriving the frame alignment every call in this function
        // depends on.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_space;
        cmp rax, 3;                      // AML_SPACE_EC
        je amr_wu_ec;

        call aml_eval_spend;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_wu_no;

        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_space;
        cmp rax, 1;                      // AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_IO
        jne amr_wu_mem;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, 4;                      // host_base
        call aml_region_row_word;
        cmp rax, 1;                      // AML_REGION_IO_REAL
        jne amr_wu_mem;

        // No 8-byte port transaction exists; see the read path.
        cmp r14, 8;
        je amr_wu_badw;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_base;
        add rax, r13;                    // port = decl_base + off
        mov rdi, rax;
        mov rsi, r14;
        mov rdx, r15;                    // value
        call aml_region_port_out;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_wu_no;
        jmp amr_wu_ok;

      amr_wu_mem:
        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, 4;
        call aml_region_row_word;
        add rax, r13;
        mov r10, rax;                    // the one address

        cmp r14, 1;
        je amr_wu_b;
        cmp r14, 2;
        je amr_wu_w;
        cmp r14, 4;
        je amr_wu_d;
        cmp r14, 8;
        je amr_wu_q;

        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        jmp amr_wu_no;

      amr_wu_badw:
        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        jmp amr_wu_no;

        // R31 wires the EC write transaction in at this label. Until then
        // a write to EC space performs NOTHING and says so.
      amr_wu_ec:
        lea r10, [rip + aml_region_ec_state];
        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 16], rax;             // gated refusals
        mov rdi, 67;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_EC_GATED
        call aml_eval_set_err;
        jmp amr_wu_no;

      amr_wu_b:
        mov_b [r10 + 0], r15;
        jmp amr_wu_ok;
      amr_wu_w:
        mov_w [r10 + 0], r15;
        jmp amr_wu_ok;
      amr_wu_d:
        mov_d [r10 + 0], r15;
        jmp amr_wu_ok;
      amr_wu_q:
        mov [r10 + 0], r15;

      amr_wu_ok:
        mov rax, 1;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_wu_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_acc_bits(field_elem_node) -> 8 | 16 | 32 | 64 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_acc_bits : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) (space: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062), made SPACE-AWARE by #1063 / #1064 / #1065: resolve the DECLARED AccessType of a field element to the transaction width every unit access of that field must use, IN THE SPACE THE REGION LIVES IN. #1062 predicted this would become a table rather than a branch and it has; the space argument is the second index. Rows -- AnyAcc 0: 8 for memory, 8 for I/O, 8 for EC, but 32 FOR PCI_CONFIG, because dword is the natural and universally safe unit of configuration space and every register that matters is dword-aligned. ByteAcc 1: 8 everywhere. WordAcc 2 / DWordAcc 3: 16 / 32 everywhere EXCEPT EC. QWordAcc 4: 64 for SystemMemory ONLY. THE THREE REFUSALS ARE THE POINT. (a) QWordAcc on SystemIO is refused because there is no 8-byte port instruction -- x86 has in/out at 1, 2 and 4 bytes and nothing wider -- so servicing it would mean splitting one declared transaction into two, and two reads of a port are two transactions, which on a FIFO or a clear-on-read status register is a different and destructive operation. It is refused, NOT silently split. (b) QWordAcc on PCI_Config is refused for the same structural reason: config space is defined in dwords and a 64-bit config transaction does not exist. (c) EC admits ONLY byte access, because the EC protocol transfers exactly one byte per handshake (ACPI 6.5 sec 12.3) -- a WordAcc EC field is a table bug, and emulating it as two byte transactions would issue two EC handshakes for one declared access. BufferAcc 5 and every unknown value are 0 in every space. A 0 return is refused by the caller as AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH. r12=space, r13=access type. Reserved labels: amr_ab_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rsi;                    // space
        call aml_field_access_type;      // rdi still holds the node
        mov r13, rax;

        cmp r13, 5;
        jae amr_ab_no;                   // BufferAcc and anything unknown

        cmp r12, 3;                      // AML_SPACE_EC
        je amr_ab_ec;
        cmp r12, 1;                      // AML_SPACE_SYSTEM_IO
        je amr_ab_io;
        cmp r12, 2;                      // AML_SPACE_PCI_CONFIG
        je amr_ab_pci;

        // SystemMemory — the only space with a 64-bit transaction.
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_ab_8;
        cmp r13, 1;
        je amr_ab_8;
        cmp r13, 2;
        je amr_ab_16;
        cmp r13, 3;
        je amr_ab_32;
        cmp r13, 4;
        je amr_ab_64;
        jmp amr_ab_no;

      amr_ab_ec:
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_ab_8;                     // AnyAcc
        cmp r13, 1;
        je amr_ab_8;                     // ByteAcc
        jmp amr_ab_no;                   // the EC has no wider transaction

      amr_ab_io:
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_ab_8;                     // AnyAcc -> the narrowest
        cmp r13, 1;
        je amr_ab_8;
        cmp r13, 2;
        je amr_ab_16;
        cmp r13, 3;
        je amr_ab_32;
        jmp amr_ab_no;                   // QWordAcc: no such port access

      amr_ab_pci:
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_ab_32;                    // AnyAcc -> dword, config space's unit
        cmp r13, 1;
        je amr_ab_8;
        cmp r13, 2;
        je amr_ab_16;
        cmp r13, 3;
        je amr_ab_32;
        jmp amr_ab_no;                   // QWordAcc: no such config access

      amr_ab_8:
        mov rax, 8;
        jmp amr_ab_out;
      amr_ab_16:
        mov rax, 16;
        jmp amr_ab_out;
      amr_ab_32:
        mov rax, 32;
        jmp amr_ab_out;
      amr_ab_64:
        mov rax, 64;
        jmp amr_ab_out;
      amr_ab_no:
        xor rax, rax;

      amr_ab_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_acc_log(aw_bits) -> 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_acc_log : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (aw: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): log2 of the access width in bits. The field loops divide a bit offset by the access width on every iteration; every legal width is a power of two, so the division is a shift, and computing the shift ONCE outside the loop rather than dividing inside it keeps the loop free of the one operation whose cost varies with its operands. Returns 0 for a width this module does not admit, which the callers have already refused. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: amr_al_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;
        je amr_al_3;
        cmp rdi, 16;
        je amr_al_4;
        cmp rdi, 32;
        je amr_al_5;
        cmp rdi, 64;
        je amr_al_6;
        xor rax, rax;
        ret;
      amr_al_3:
        mov rax, 3;
        ret;
      amr_al_4:
        mov rax, 4;
        ret;
      amr_al_5:
        mov rax, 5;
        ret;
      amr_al_6:
        mov rax, 6;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_of_field(field_elem_node) -> opregion node | 0
  //
  // Walks a FIELD_ELEM up to its enclosing Field declaration and
  // resolves the region that declaration names.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_of_field : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the OPREGION node a field element belongs to, 0 with a latched code otherwise. A FIELD_ELEM's parent is its FIELD declaration, and it is the FIELD that carries the REGION's NameString -- #1054 established that FIELD/INDEXFIELD/BANKFIELD are name-TRANSPARENT for exactly this reason, so the name on the parent is the region's and not the field's. The name is resolved from the scope CONTAINING the Field declaration (the FIELD node's own parent), because that is the scope the region name was written in. INDEXFIELD (12) and BANKFIELD (13) are refused with AML_ERR_REGION_INDIRECT_FIELD (64): they name a two-step protocol -- write an index or bank register, then access a data register -- and treating their elements as direct offsets into a region would read a completely different register while looking entirely successful. That refusal is the honest statement of where this milestone stops. A parent that is not a field declaration at all is AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND. Locals r12=node, r13=field decl. Reserved labels: amr_of_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_of_unbound;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 14;                     // AML_NODE_FIELD_ELEM
        jne amr_of_unbound;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_parent;
        mov r13, rax;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_of_unbound;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 12;                     // INDEXFIELD
        je amr_of_indirect;
        cmp rax, 13;                     // BANKFIELD
        je amr_of_indirect;
        cmp rax, 11;                     // FIELD
        jne amr_of_unbound;

        // Scope containing the Field declaration.
        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_parent;
        mov r14, rax;

        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_node_name;
        mov r15, rax;
        cmp r15, 0;
        je amr_of_unbound;

        mov rdi, r14;
        mov rsi, r15;
        call aml_eval_resolve;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_of_fail;                  // aml_eval_resolve latched already
        mov r14, rax;

        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_node_kind;
        cmp rax, 10;                     // OPREGION
        jne amr_of_unbound;

        mov rax, r14;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_of_indirect:
        mov rdi, 64;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_INDIRECT_FIELD
        jmp amr_of_latch;
      amr_of_unbound:
        mov rdi, 58;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND
      amr_of_latch:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
      amr_of_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_field_binding(field_elem_node) -> b | 0
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_field_binding : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): the binding a field element resolves through, 0 with a latched code otherwise. Chains aml_region_of_field then aml_region_find then aml_region_row_live. THE LIVENESS CHECK IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH THE FIND: find matches on the node alone, and the only rows whose node is non-zero are rows aml_region_bind wrote -- but stating the liveness requirement HERE, at the single point every access path passes through, is what makes 'a region that was never granted a capability' impossible to reach the arithmetic through, rather than merely unlikely to. An unbound region is AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND (58), which is the code that means 'the supervisor holds no capability covering this region'. Reserved labels: amr_fb_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        call aml_region_of_field;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fb_fail;                  // already latched

        mov rdi, rax;
        call aml_region_find;
        mov r12, rax;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je amr_fb_unbound;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_row_live;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fb_unbound;

        mov rax, r12;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_fb_unbound:
        mov rdi, 58;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND
        call aml_eval_set_err;
      amr_fb_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_field_read(field_elem_node) -> u64
  //
  // Assemble a bit-granular, possibly unaligned field from aligned
  // access units at the DECLARED width.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_field_read : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): read a FieldUnit. Refusals first: a field of zero width or wider than 64 bits is AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH (61) -- ACPI makes a >64-bit field read as a BUFFER, and truncating it to 64 bits would return a plausible wrong answer, so it is refused until the Buffer-valued form lands; a binding that does not exist is AML_ERR_REGION_UNBOUND; an access type this space cannot service is AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH. The walk: cur = bit_offset, end = bit_offset + bit_width; each iteration takes the unit containing `cur`, computes lo = cur - unit_base_bit and n = min(access_width - lo, end - cur), READS THE WHOLE UNIT AT THE DECLARED WIDTH (never a narrower access that happens to cover the bits), extracts bits [lo, lo+n) and appends them at the running output position. A unit whose aligned extent leaves the region is refused by aml_region_bounds_ok as AML_ERR_REGION_OOB -- straddling is an error, not a narrower access. Frame: [rsp+0] cur, +8 end, +16 out, +24 outbit, +32 lo, +40 n, +48 unit_base_bit, +56 mask. r12=node, r13=binding, r14=access width in bits, r15=log2 of it. Reserved labels: amr_fr_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 64;

        mov r12, rdi;

        // -- Field extent.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg0;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;              // cur = bit offset
        mov r10, rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg1;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fr_badw;
        cmp rax, 64;
        ja amr_fr_badw;
        mov r11, [rsp + 0];
        add rax, r11;
        mov [rsp + 8], rax;              // end

        // -- Binding.
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_field_binding;
        mov r13, rax;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_fr_fail;

        // -- Declared access width, RESOLVED IN THIS REGION'S SPACE. The
        //    binding is looked up first precisely so the space is known
        //    here: QWordAcc is legal over SystemMemory and refused over
        //    every other space, so the width cannot be resolved from the
        //    field declaration alone.
        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_region_space;
        mov rsi, rax;
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_acc_bits;
        mov r14, rax;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_fr_badacc;
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_region_acc_log;
        mov r15, rax;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rsp + 16], rax;             // out
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;             // outbit

      amr_fr_loop:
        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];
        cmp rax, r10;
        jae amr_fr_done;

        // unit_base_bit = (cur >> log) << log
        mov r11, rax;
        mov rcx, r15;
        shr r11, cl;
        shl r11, cl;
        mov [rsp + 48], r11;

        // lo = cur - unit_base_bit
        mov r8, rax;
        sub r8, r11;
        mov [rsp + 32], r8;

        // n = min(aw - lo, end - cur)
        mov r9, r14;
        sub r9, r8;
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];
        sub r10, rax;
        cmp r9, r10;
        jbe amr_fr_n_ok;
        mov r9, r10;
      amr_fr_n_ok:
        mov [rsp + 40], r9;

        mov rdi, r9;
        call aml_region_mask;
        mov [rsp + 56], rax;

        // Bounds-check the ALIGNED unit before reading it.
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;                      // unit byte offset
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;                      // unit byte width
        call aml_region_bounds_ok;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fr_fail;

        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;
        call aml_region_read_unit;

        mov rcx, [rsp + 32];             // lo
        shr rax, cl;
        mov r10, [rsp + 56];             // mask
        and rax, r10;
        mov rcx, [rsp + 24];             // outbit
        shl rax, cl;
        mov r10, [rsp + 16];
        or  r10, rax;
        mov [rsp + 16], r10;

        mov rax, [rsp + 24];
        mov r10, [rsp + 40];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;             // outbit += n

        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;              // cur += n
        jmp amr_fr_loop;

      amr_fr_done:
        mov rax, [rsp + 16];
        add rsp, 64;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_fr_badw:
        mov rdi, 61;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH
        jmp amr_fr_latch;
      amr_fr_badacc:
        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
      amr_fr_latch:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
      amr_fr_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        add rsp, 64;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_field_store(field_elem_node, value) -> 1 | 0
  //
  // The write half, with the UpdateRule deciding the fate of the
  // neighbouring bits.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_field_store : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) (value: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): write a FieldUnit. Same refusals and the same unit walk as the read. Per unit: m = mask(n) << lo is the field's footprint inside this unit and newbits = ((value >> outbit) & mask(n)) << lo are the bits going in. THE UPDATERULE (§19.5.5.2) THEN DECIDES THE REST OF THE UNIT. Preserve (0) READS THE UNIT AND MERGES: (unit & ~m) | newbits -- this is the read-modify-write, and it is what stops a 3-bit field at bit 5 of a byte from clearing the other five bits, which on real hardware are five other control bits. WriteAsOnes (1) writes (~m) | newbits and WriteAsZeros (2) writes newbits, and CRUCIALLY NEITHER READS FIRST: those rules exist precisely to name registers where reading has a side effect -- clear-on-read status, FIFO pop -- and an implementation that read anyway to be safe would corrupt the hardware the rule was written to protect. Frame: [rsp+0] cur, +8 end, +16 value, +24 outbit, +32 lo, +40 n, +48 unit_base_bit, +56 mask, +64 m, +72 newbits, +80 update rule. r12=node, r13=binding, r14=access width bits, r15=log2. Reserved labels: amr_fw_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 96;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov [rsp + 16], rsi;             // value

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg0;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;              // cur

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_node_arg1;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fw_badw;
        cmp rax, 64;
        ja amr_fw_badw;
        mov r11, [rsp + 0];
        add rax, r11;
        mov [rsp + 8], rax;              // end

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_field_binding;
        mov r13, rax;
        cmp r13, 0;
        je amr_fw_fail;

        // Space-resolved width; see the read path for why the binding
        // must be known before the width can be.
        mov rdi, r13;
        call aml_region_space;
        mov rsi, rax;
        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_region_acc_bits;
        mov r14, rax;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je amr_fw_badacc;
        mov rdi, r14;
        call aml_region_acc_log;
        mov r15, rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_field_update_rule;
        mov [rsp + 80], rax;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;             // outbit

      amr_fw_loop:
        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];
        cmp rax, r10;
        jae amr_fw_done;

        mov r11, rax;
        mov rcx, r15;
        shr r11, cl;
        shl r11, cl;
        mov [rsp + 48], r11;             // unit_base_bit

        mov r8, rax;
        sub r8, r11;
        mov [rsp + 32], r8;              // lo

        mov r9, r14;
        sub r9, r8;
        mov r10, [rsp + 8];
        sub r10, rax;
        cmp r9, r10;
        jbe amr_fw_n_ok;
        mov r9, r10;
      amr_fw_n_ok:
        mov [rsp + 40], r9;              // n

        mov rdi, r9;
        call aml_region_mask;
        mov [rsp + 56], rax;             // mask

        // m = mask << lo
        mov rcx, [rsp + 32];
        shl rax, cl;
        mov [rsp + 64], rax;

        // newbits = ((value >> outbit) & mask) << lo
        mov rax, [rsp + 16];
        mov rcx, [rsp + 24];
        shr rax, cl;
        mov r10, [rsp + 56];
        and rax, r10;
        mov rcx, [rsp + 32];
        shl rax, cl;
        mov [rsp + 72], rax;

        // Bounds-check the aligned unit before touching it.
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;
        call aml_region_bounds_ok;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fw_fail;

        // Fast path: the field covers the WHOLE unit, so no rule applies
        // and no read is needed under any of the three.
        mov rax, [rsp + 40];
        cmp rax, r14;
        je amr_fw_whole;

        mov rax, [rsp + 80];
        cmp rax, 1;
        je amr_fw_ones;
        cmp rax, 2;
        je amr_fw_zeros;

        // -- Preserve: READ-MODIFY-WRITE.
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;
        call aml_region_read_unit;
        mov r10, [rsp + 64];             // m
        mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        xor r10, r11;                    // ~m
        and rax, r10;                    // keep the neighbours
        mov r10, [rsp + 72];
        or  rax, r10;                    // insert the field
        jmp amr_fw_put;

      amr_fw_ones:
        mov r10, [rsp + 64];
        mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        xor r10, r11;                    // ~m
        mov rax, r10;
        mov r10, [rsp + 72];
        or  rax, r10;
        jmp amr_fw_put;

      amr_fw_zeros:
        mov rax, [rsp + 72];
        jmp amr_fw_put;

      amr_fw_whole:
        mov rax, [rsp + 72];

      amr_fw_put:
        mov rdi, r13;
        mov rsi, [rsp + 48];
        shr rsi, 3;
        mov rdx, r14;
        shr rdx, 3;
        mov rcx, rax;
        call aml_region_write_unit;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je amr_fw_fail;

        mov rax, [rsp + 24];
        mov r10, [rsp + 40];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [rsp + 24], rax;

        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        add rax, r10;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;
        jmp amr_fw_loop;

      amr_fw_done:
        mov rax, 1;
        add rsp, 96;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_fw_badw:
        mov rdi, 61;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_FIELD_WIDTH
        jmp amr_fw_latch;
      amr_fw_badacc:
        mov rdi, 60;                     // AML_ERR_REGION_ACCESS_WIDTH
      amr_fw_latch:
        call aml_eval_set_err;
      amr_fw_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        add rsp, 96;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_field_load_obj(field_elem_node) -> object | 0
  //
  // The object-model entry point the evaluator's read path calls.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_field_load_obj : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (node: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): read a FieldUnit and wrap the result as an Integer object, 0 with a latched code on any refusal. The wrap is a separate function from aml_region_field_read because the read's zero is a LEGITIMATE VALUE and the object path's zero is a failure: distinguishing them here, once, by consulting aml_eval_err rather than the returned value, is what stops a field that genuinely reads 0 from being reported as a refusal at every call site. A field at most 64 bits wide is by definition an Integer per ACPI; the Buffer-valued form for wider fields is refused upstream. Reserved labels: amr_lo_.",
      block: {
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        call aml_eval_err;
        mov r13, rax;                    // error state on entry

        call aml_region_field_read;
        mov r12, rax;

        call aml_eval_err;
        cmp rax, r13;
        jne amr_lo_fail;                 // a NEW error was latched

        mov rdi, r12;
        call aml_obj_int;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret;

      amr_lo_fail:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // aml_region_handles(op16) -> u64
  //
  // The executable statement of what this module services, asserted by
  // the corpus in both directions — the same device as
  // aml_arith_handles / aml_ref_handles / aml_ctl_handles.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let aml_region_handles : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (op16: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M3-002 (#1062): this module handles NO OPCODE. It is reached through the FieldUnit destination and source paths in aml_eval, not through an opcode dispatch, and saying so as an executable predicate is worth more than leaving the question open -- the sibling modules all answer this question and a reader comparing them would otherwise have to infer the answer from absence. When R30.M4 adds an explicit region-access operator this becomes a row. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: none.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

}
